  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nA dynamic low pressure system is currently making its way through\nnorthern Montana. This brought dry showers and thunderstorms with\nstrong wind gusts across south central MT this evening. Behind\nthis push of precipitation, strong subsidence and quickly rising\npressure will create breezy to strong winds across this same area.\nGusts will be strongest across the western foothills (from Big \nTimber to Livingston through Harlowton) with gusts 60-70 mph\npossible. Areas east (through Carbon, southwest Yellowstone and \nMusselshell counties) are expected to see gusts 45-60 mph. The \ntiming for the strongest winds will be through this morning, \ngenerally decreasing through the afternoon. \n\nConditions today will be cooler, but still warm and dry. With \ngreen up underway, the concern for fire spread is low. However, \ngiven fire weather conditions, people should still use caution and\nnot cause a spark. \n\nLooking at Friday, westerly flow and subsidence aloft will create\nanother breezy day with gusts mainly in the 25-40 mph range\n(strongest across east central MT). Late Saturday, ensembles bring\nin troughing, but are differing on the track of the low which\ncould make a difference on precipitation totals. Ensembles do \ngive the region beneficial precipitation with around 1+ inches\nthrough Saturday through Tuesday in the higher elevations. Across\nthe lower elevations, totals ranges from 0.4-0.8 inches. Changes \nto the forecast are likely, but there is hope for appreciable \nprecipitation next week. \n\nHighs will be in the 60s and 70s through Saturday, cooling to the\n40s and 50s through Tuesday. TS",
